[38;5;231m⏺[39m One more question before I present the design. The repo has no configured tooling — no linter, no formatter, no test
  runner — so there's currently no way to test a preferences module beyond loading the page by hand. Setting this up is
  cheapest now, before the code exists, and whatever you pick lands in the spec's constraints so later work inherits it.

[38;5;246m⏺ [39mUser answered Claude's questions:
[38;5;246m  ⎿  · Which tooling should the spec set up from the start? → Unit tests (Recommended)[39m

[38;5;231m⏺[39m Here's the design. Section 1 of 2 — module and data model.

  [1mModule.[0m A new [38;5;153mprefs.js[39m at the repo root, loaded by [38;5;153mindex.html[39m before [38;5;153mapp.js[39m. It's a classic script (not ESM) exposing
  a single [38;5;153mPrefs[39m object, with a CommonJS [38;5;153mmodule.exports[39m tail so tests can require it — that matches the existing
  [38;5;153msrc/utils.js[39m pattern and avoids converting [38;5;153mindex.html[39m to modules for one file.

  [1mOne key, one object.[0m Everything lives under a single localStorage key, [38;5;153mwebapp.preferences[39m, holding one JSON object:

  { [36m"version"[39m: [32m1[39m, [36m"rememberUsername"[39m: [34mfalse[39m, [36m"lastUsername"[39m: [31m""[39m }

  One key rather than a key per preference: reads and writes stay atomic, defaults merge in one place, the app owns one
  namespace instead of scattering entries, and [38;5;153mversion[39m gives a migration handle later. The cost is that writing one
  preference rewrites the object — irrelevant at this size.

  [1mInterface.[0m Four functions, all synchronous:

  - [38;5;153mPrefs.get(name)[39m — stored value, or the default if absent
  - [38;5;153mPrefs.set(name, value)[39m — persist; returns [38;5;153mtrue[39m if it reached disk, [38;5;153mfalse[39m if only memory
  - [38;5;153mPrefs.all()[39m — a copy of the merged object
  - [38;5;153mPrefs.reset()[39m — back to defaults

  A [38;5;153mDEFAULTS[39m constant is the single source of truth for which preferences exist and what type each is. [38;5;153mget[39m/[38;5;153mset[39m on a name
  not in [38;5;153mDEFAULTS[39m throws — that turns a typo into an immediate error instead of a silently-lost setting. A [38;5;153mset[39m whose
  value type doesn't match the default's type is rejected the same way.

  Does the module and data model look right before I go on to integration, failure handling, and tests?

